        "text": "Essential Services Commission Guideline: Payment Difficulty Framework 22\nBetter practice\nThe commission considers it better practice for energy retailers to ensure their staff are\nadequately trained so that they understand the eligibility criteria for an account holder to apply\nfor a Utility Relief Grant.\nThe eligibility criteria for the Utility Relief Grant scheme can be found on the Department of\nFamilies, Fairness and Housing webpage .\nNon-compliance with the obligation\nFailure to provide information relating to the Utility Relief Grant scheme or failure to assist\ncustomers to complete a Utility Relief Grant application over the phone is a breach of the energy\nretailers’ obligations under clause 141 of the code of practice. This clause is specified as a civil\npenalty requirement for the purposes of the Essential Services Commission Act 2001 .\nBetter practice examples\n• Monitor a customer’s eligibility for a Utility Relief Grant every two years and for the availability\nto ‘top up’ applications until the maximum available grant is reached within the eligible period\nfor both electricity and gas (where relevant).\n• Monitor customer entitlement to concessions whenever reasonably practicable.\n• At a minimum, monitor customer entitlement to concessions when entering or switching a\ncustomer to a new contract, or when a customer requests standard or tailored assistance,\nand as soon as practicable for customers affected by family violence.\n• The commission considers it better practice for energy retailers to arrange backdating of\nconcessions where customers are eligible. 30\n• Obtain explicit informed consent to obtain updated details directly from Centrelink\nConfirmation eServices .\n• Add information about the availability of a Utility Relief Grant onto all customer\ncommunications.\n30 ‘Concessions & benefits’ Department of Family, Fairness and Housing https://services.dffh.vic.gov.au/energy as at 20\nNovember 2023."
